Sub forms:


- 🔥Sometimes you don’t want to define the entire form in a single component. You may want to split it
    into several sub-forms, each defined in its own component. 🔥With signal forms, this is easy to
    do by passing a field to the child component.

    👉Let’s say our user form has an address sub-form:
    register.ts:

        protected readonly user = signal({
            firstname: '',
            lastname: '',
            address: {
                number: '',
                street: '',
                zipcode: '',
                city: ''
            }
        });

        protected readonly userForm = form(
            this.user,
            f => {
                required(f.firstname, { message: 'First name is required' });
                required(f.lastname, { message: 'Last name is required' });
                //👇 the address schema can be defined separately and reused
                apply(f.address, addressSchema);
            },
            {
                submission: {
                    action: async () => await this.save()
                }
            }
        );
    

    👉We can define an Address component that accepts a FieldTree for the address:
    address.ts:

        export class AddressSubForm {
            readonly addressField = input.required<FieldTree<AddressModel>>();
        }


    👉Then we can use our Address component in the Register template:
    register.html:

        <ns-address-sub-form 👉[addressField]="userForm.address" />
    

    👉In the template, we can then 🔥use the Field directive to bind the inputs to the fields of
    the address:
    address.html:

        @let address = addressField();
        <div>
            @let number = address.number;
            <label for="number">Number</label>
            <input id="number" [formField]="number" />
            @if (number().touched() && number().invalid()) {
                <div>
                    @for (error of number().errors(); track error.kind) {
                        <div>{{ error.message }}</div>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    

    That makes it really easy to split a form into several components!